---
title: 'Why Bother?'
description: "On chasing what's yours after you've already paid for what was expected of you."
date: 2026-07-27
kind: note
draft: false
---

Seriously, why bother?

It's a question I've always shied away from, because it's a harder question than it sounds. There are two ways to ask it. One is aimed outward, at what the world expects. The other is aimed inward, at the years and effort you've already spent trying to meet it, and whether more of the same is really the honest choice.

Curiosity was never the risk. It was the one thing that stayed constant enough to trust. What kept it from being blind wasn't caution instead of curiosity. It was choosing where to aim it, deciding what was actually worth the weight of following.

Sometimes meeting what's expected isn't a choice at all. It's just fear, dressed up as one: the fear of finding out the years already spent meant less than you needed them to.

So why bother? Because those years were never a debt owed to what was expected of me. They were the price of finding out it wasn't mine to begin with. Meeting that expectation now wouldn't make the first payment worth more. It would just mean paying it twice.
